From 28a4f632e2ee8d37743aca5742230a3e50d1e8d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:09:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] A page per child, and a generator that will not write
 broken JavaScript

THE ROLL NO LONGER OPENS A DRAWER. The masjid asked for what their old
system does: clicking a pupil opens that pupil's page. portal/pupil/?id=
is the first deep-linked record page in this portal, so whatever it
establishes, Staff and Families will copy.

THE URL IS NOT THE ACCESS CONTROL. The id is an opaque UUID, but that is
not why this is safe - madrasah_pupil_one() is, and it still demands
two-step and still writes an audit row. Worth saying plainly, because the
temptation with a deep link is to treat a hard-to-guess id as protection.

THE PAGE TITLE CARRIES NO CHILD'S NAME. Browser history on a shared
office computer is a list of who was looked at, and the title is what
history keeps. The name is in the h1. The generator refuses to write a
page whose title is not the nameless one.

ONE AUDIT ROW PER PUPIL, NOT ONE PER TAB - asserted after four tab
changes. Opening the child is the auditable act; the tabs arrange what
was already fetched. Five tabs writing five rows would make the trail
useless for answering "who read this child's record".

THE MEDICAL BOX IS NOT BEHIND A TAB. An allergy is safety information and
a teacher needs it the moment the page opens. It sits above the tabs and
stays on screen whichever one is chosen, on the phone too.

THE 270 LINES THE RECORD USED TO OCCUPY ARE DELETED, not commented out
and not merely unreachable. Two copies of a form that writes a child's
medical note is one copy too many, and the one nobody runs is the one
that rots.

A SCREEN THAT LOADS IS NOT A SCREEN THAT WORKS. One mismatched quote -
a string opened with ' and closed with " - produced a page that fetched
its shell, drew the masthead and the rail, and then did nothing at all,
because the script died before the module was defined. It looked like a
slow network. Thirteen checks passed against it. Both generators now run
`node --check` on what they are about to write, BEFORE writing it: the
first version wrote, checked, then deleted on failure, which turned a
typo into a 404 instead of leaving the last good build alone. Verified by
reintroducing the quote: exit 1, nothing written, app.js untouched.

34 checks on the new page, 86 on the roll.

