@@ -810,6 +810,7 @@ Migrations are pasted into the SQL editor in order.
 | `035_notify_the_other_three_forms` | **Three more forms that wrote a row and told nobody**, found by asking of every anon-callable function "and then who is told?" Madrasah admissions, course registrations and foodbank volunteers all collected an email address and never used it. Adds their webhooks the same way `033` does, plus `grant select on courses to service_role` so a course key becomes its real name. The madrasah email deliberately carries **nothing at all about a child** — no name, date of birth, school, SEND, EHCP, allergy or medical detail. |
 | `050_the_app_can_publish_a_notice_again` | **A production outage this project caused, and did not notice.** `040` dropped `publish_notice()` — rightly; it had no admin check at all and was safe only by its grant. What nobody asked was who else was calling it. The phone app's Cloudflare Worker was, and it sends the push *after* writing the row, so from that morning **pressing "Send notification" on the app's trustee screen failed and no notification went out either**. Restores it with the same name, argument and return shape the Worker expects, service_role only, and validating through `check_notice()` — so the app and the website have one definition of a valid notice and two doors to it. |
 | `076_somebody_has_to_be_able_to_read_an_application` | **The admissions form had been live and nobody at the masjid could open what it wrote.** 008 built the whole pipeline and stopped one function short: a parent applies, the office is emailed, the row is purged three years later, and in between there is no list, no record and no decision — `admission_applications` is FORCE RLS with no grant, so the table is sealed. It had never shown up because nobody had applied yet. Adds the overview, the list, the record and the decision. The **list carries flags and never detail** — a check fails if it ever learns `send_detail`, `allergy_detail` or `medical_conditions` — and **opening one child's record writes an audit row**, because "who has read this" is a question a subject access request asks. Also revokes the direct `SELECT` 008 gave `authenticated`: the policy behind it required `verified_admin()` so nothing was ever exposed, but an administrator could read every application through PostgREST without ever calling the function that writes down who looked, and an audit trail with a documented bypass is worse than none. |
+| `079_status_export_and_a_guard_that_runs` | **A pupil can be on hold, an administrator can take a file out, and the rule that stops a list learning a child's medical note now actually runs.** Three things. **Status**: the madrasah could record two states, left or not, so a child who had stopped for a term had nowhere to be except *on roll*. Four states, with a constraint tying `left` to a leaving date so the two can never disagree, and a check that tries three wrong combinations before trusting it. The old system's eleven statuses are **not** copied - four of them are *application* states and Admissions owns those here, so nine would sit permanently at zero and a filter that is always empty teaches people to stop reading the filters. **Export**: the highest-risk feature in the portal, because everything else keeps children inside a screen and this puts 552 of them in a file that gets emailed. Two levels - a four-column register list for any staff member, a thirteen-column list for administrators - **and an audit row every time** naming who, when, which columns, how many rows and the filter in force, so *exported the whole roll* and *exported one class of twelve* are different entries. Neither carries medical, allergies or SEND. Proved end to end against the live register in a rolled-back transaction. **The guard**: 077 asserted that `madrasah_roll()` does not return a detail column and the note afterwards claimed the split *"cannot be undone by somebody adding just one field"*. **That was false** - the assertion ran once, at migration time, in a block long since finished; nothing re-ran it. It now lives in `health_check()`. Its first version **failed correct code**, flagging the four legitimate `p.medical is not null` marks, so the rule became *every mention must be immediately followed by IS NULL* - test it freely, return it and this fails. And the check that proved the guard originally did so by replacing the live export with a stub returning everyone's medical notes and relying on rollback; it now points at its own throwaway. Reading health for the first time also revealed the four `import_` landing tables had been failing it since this morning - exempted from the tenancy check and given one of their own that says out loud they are still holding a second copy of the register. |
 | `078_what_the_register_got_wrong` | **The numbers reconciled and the register was still wrong about nine children.** 077 proved 552 pupils, 554 class places and 330 families all tallied; three sanity checks afterwards asked the harder question. Two spaces did the first damage: the register spells three class names with a double space where the September load used one, so the import did not recognise them and made a SECOND copy of each - the roll went to the new copy and the old sat empty beside it. Nothing was lost and the counts stayed right, which is precisely why it survived 077; a staff list showing *Boys Hafiz Class - C* twice, one of them empty, is how somebody marks a register against the wrong one. Merged, spacing normalised so the next import matches, and the four classes the register never mentions made inactive rather than deleted. Then nine dates of birth that cannot be right: a 51 year old in Boys Year 6, three 3 year olds in Ladies Naazirah, a four year old in Boys Year 8. **The obvious detectors are all wrong and each looked right until it was run** - "far from the class median" fires 41 times, 37 of them adult learners where a 21-to-64 spread is correct; "anyone under five" fires 55 times, 53 of them on Play and Pray and Reception, which EXIST for three and four year olds; "outside the class's own range" cannot catch three 3 year olds who together widen the range being measured. Three plain tests in three directions do it, and a check fails if the rule ever flags a correct infant enrolment. Nothing corrects a date - only a parent can settle one - the screen finds them. Also: **a pupil's own e-mail now counts as a contact**, because three adult students in the Ladies classes have no guardian (they ARE the adult) and the roll was calling them unreachable while holding their address. "No way to ring" fell from 10 to 3. |
 | `077_the_register_552_pupils` | **The madrasah's real roll: 552 children, 330 families, 44 classes, 37 teachers.** The pupil record held a first name, a last name, two dates, a family and a fee rate, so there was nowhere for a date of birth, an address, a school, a medical note or an allergy — which is most of what the register holds. Fifteen columns, a unique pupil reference so the import is repeatable, and the same split Applications uses: `madrasah_roll()` returns marks and never detail (a check reads the function's own source and fails if it learns a detail column), while `madrasah_pupil_one()` returns the detail and **writes down who read it** — deliberately not `stable`, because the planner may elide a stable call and the audit row would vanish. **Ethnicity is deliberately absent**: blank in the sound export, unreadable in the damaged one, and Article 9 data besides — a column sitting empty is an invitation to fill it. An earlier load on 18 September had put 543 pupils in with no reference number, which the import could not have matched: **every child would have appeared twice**. So the import clears what it cannot match and then checks its own result, raising — and rolling back — if the numbers disagree. The first version of that guard compared the file against the madrasah *after* clearing, which can never fail; its own check caught it. The register's 552 children are uploaded through the Supabase dashboard and never pass through the assistant. |
 | `075_the_reconciler_could_never_have_run` | **It threw every time it had work to do.** 072's reconciler wrote an audit row with `masjid_id` null into a NOT NULL column, after the loop — so the first reminder it resolved raised 23502 and rolled back every outcome it had just written. Reminders would have sat at `queued` for ever, holding the seven-day lock, and the six-hour rule that turns silence into an honest failure is inside the same aborted loop. Green every five minutes since 20 September purely because nothing was ever queued for it. Its check now RUNS the function against all three answers a mail server can give. |
